# Doshell — Installation Guide

Doshell brings familiar DOS/Windows-style commands to Linux shells. Whether you're
on WSL, Ubuntu, Fedora, Arch, or any Bash-compatible system, it bridges the
muscle-memory gap with aliases, functions, and honest guidance for commands that
belong to Linux.

---

## Installation

```bash
git clone https://github.com/twbaty/doshell.git
cd doshell
chmod +x setup-doshell.sh
./setup-doshell.sh --install
```

Reload your shell when done:

```bash
source ~/.bashrc   # or source ~/.zshrc
```

Verify it's working:

```bash
dir          # should behave like ls -l
cls          # should clear the screen
tasklist     # should show running processes
```

---

## Installer Options

| Flag | What it does |
|------|-------------|
| `--install` | Install aliases and optional dependencies |
| `--uninstall` | Remove all doshell aliases and clean up |
| `--reinstall` | `git pull` + uninstall + reinstall |
| `--version` | Show version, log summary, installed deps |
| `--dry-run` | Preview all changes without applying them |
| `--verbose` | Show each command as it runs |
| `-y` / `--yes` | Skip all confirmation prompts |
| `--help` | Full usage information |

---

## Optional Dependencies

During install you'll be asked whether to install optional tools:

- `tree` — supports the `tree` alias
- `traceroute` — supports `tracert`
- `dnsutils` (`dig`) — supports `nslookup`
- `nano` — supports `edit` and `notepad` fallback
- `fzf` — available for future interactive features

The installer detects your package manager (`apt`, `yum`, `dnf`) automatically.

---

## Shell Compatibility

Doshell writes aliases to `~/.bash_aliases` and adds a source line to your shell's
rc file (`~/.bashrc`, `~/.zshrc`, or `~/.profile`). It is tested on:

- Bash (primary)
- Zsh
- Dash / Ash

For any other shell, source the file manually:

```bash
source ~/.bash_aliases
```

---

## Uninstalling

```bash
./setup-doshell.sh --uninstall
```

This removes the doshell alias block from `~/.bash_aliases`, removes the source
line from your rc file, and optionally removes installed dependencies and the log.

